---
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<figure class="plate">
  <img src={u("/art/perinusa-mills.webp")}
       alt="A constructed illustration: a long row of stone watermills with arched tailraces and paddle wheels along a clear river in a bare karst plain, sacks of grain stacked under low stone-slab roofs, mules on the bank, a many-arched stone causeway crossing the river beside them, poplars along the banks, a plain stone manor house with a walled garden behind, and a hot bare mountain wall in the distance."
       width="1168" height="725" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">
    <strong>The mills at Perinuša.</strong> A constructed illustration, made for this archive by an image model working
    from a written brief. It is illustration and not evidence; the figures are distant and none has a face.
    <br /><br />
    The documents give the shape of it. The begluk of the arci-beg Čaušević came with eight mills on the Vrljika; Zuane's
    son Antonio got Venetian leave to build six more, and a Venetian cadastral sheet of 1777 draws the
    «Ponte di pietra» and the «Molinari di Franceschi» with eleven grindstones — fourteen in the end. The causeway ran on
    thirteen piers, and when the Austrians rebuilt them at the start of the twentieth century each pier head was set at a
    different angle to the water pressure, so that every wheel turned with the same force however far it stood from the
    main stream. Men came from as far as Duvno and waited up to ten days for their grain, sleeping on their own sacks
    under those low stone roofs. The mills ran until the end of the twentieth century.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .plate { margin: 0; }
  .plate img { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 720px; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
